    {
      "tvm_method_id": 70901,
      "name": "partnerBpsOf",
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "partner",
          "ty_idx": 27
        }
      ],
      "return_ty_idx": 1,
      "description": "R23-7. `addr_none` IS A FIRST-CLASS ADDRESS AND MUST NOT UNDERFLOW.\n\n`getWorkchainAndHash()` reads a workchain and a 256-bit hash, so it throws\ncell-underflow (exit 9, uncatalogued) on `addr_none$00` — which is a legal\n`MsgAddress` arm and is exactly what `pendingAdmin()` returns as its null. A\nclient that round-trips one of this contract's own getter outputs back into\nanother of its getters got exit 9. Measured pre-fix on both this and\n`referrerRate`.\n\n0 is the correct answer on its merits: `addr_none` is not a registered partner\nand never can be, so its share is zero.\nDeclared `any_address` on purpose: the parameter's DECLARED type does not\nconstrain what a caller actually pushes, so typing it `address` only hid the\narm that underflows. Accepting the wider type is what makes the guard\nexpressible at all, and it does not change the ABI a well-formed caller sees."
    },
    {
      "tvm_method_id": 106222,
      "name": "pendingNftCollection",
      "parameters": [],
      "return_ty_idx": 21,
      "description": "R24-4: staged state must be readable, or the handshake buys no warning time —\nthe same rule R7-11 applied to every other staged field in this system. A\ndeploy script, a monitor or an operator can see exactly which pointer is\nwaiting for its acknowledgement, and `nftCollection()` still reports the live\none (null while unconfirmed)."
    },
    {
      "tvm_method_id": 85907,
      "name": "stateBudget",
      "parameters": [],
      "return_ty_idx": 28,
      "description": "R24-5: the account's own cell budget, reported rather than merely enforced.\n\nFollows the arc getgems took between auction v3r3 and v4r1 — v3r3 THREW at\ntooling when its config was unsound, v4r1 REPORTS the same condition as a\ngetter field and throws only where money is at stake. A monitor can alarm on\n`used` approaching `ceiling` weeks before any user sees a refusal, and the\nthree population counts make it obvious WHICH one is growing.\n\n(used, ceiling, hardWall, names, commitments, lots)"
    },
    {
      "tvm_method_id": 85950,
      "name": "referralBps",
      "parameters": [],
      "return_ty_idx": 1
    },
    {
      "tvm_method_id": 65560,
      "name": "referrerRate",
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "referrer",
          "ty_idx": 27
        }
      ],
      "return_ty_idx": 1,
      "description": "R23-7 sibling — see partnerBpsOf. An `addr_none` argument underflowed here too.\nAnswering with the PUBLIC rate rather than 0: an unknown referrer already falls\nback to `cfg.referralBps` in `effectiveReferralBps`, and `addr_none` is simply\nan unknown referrer, so this keeps the getter's contract (\"what rate would this\naddress get\") uniform instead of inventing a second meaning for one arm."
    },
